  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nSurface high pressure exits to the northeast early this morning as a \nstrong low pressure system moves up the east coast into the eastern \nOhio Valley by this afternoon. Being on the northern periphery of \nthe low in the deformation zone will bring widespread stratiform \nrain from the south to north. Rain will begin across Central Ohio \nearly Thursday afternoon and spread northward into the evening. The \nheaviest portion of the rain will occur during the afternoon and \nearly evening today. As the surface low moves into the eastern Ohio \nValley, the rain will begin to lighten up as the main moisture band \nmoves into the eastern Great Lakes. Rainfall is still expected \nthrough Friday evening as there will be some wrap around moisture \naround the low. As winds shift around to the northwest behind the \nlow, there will be some lake effect showers across NE OH and NW PA \ninto early Saturday. 850mb temperatures will drop down to -2 to -4C \nduring this time, and some rain/snow mix to wet snow is possible \nwith the lake effect. A low level ridge will begin to nudge in to \nthe western Great Lakes late Friday, and winds will gradually shift \nto be out of the west. This will cut off the lake effect by Saturday \nmorning. Through the event, rainfall totals will be between 1.00-\n1.50 inches with the highest totals in NE OH and NW PA. \n\nTo accompany the rainfall, as the surface low deepens wind gusts \nwill begin to increase out of the northeast this afternoon. The \nstrongest winds are expected in NW OH downwind from Lake Erie \npeaking near 50 mph this evening. A Wind Advisory has been issued \nfor Lucas, Wood, Ottawa, Sandusky, and Erie from this afternoon \nthrough early Friday morning. Elsewhere, wind gusts will be between \n30-40 mph throughout the day. With the northeast wind flow, water \nlevels will rise in the western basin and coastal flooding will be \nan issue from Lucas to Erie County. A Lakeshore Flood Warning has \nbeen issued for these counties from this morning morning through \nearly Friday morning. Impacts will begin to lessen as the winds \nshift around to the northwest. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nThere will be a brief period of dry weather through much of the day \nSaturday through the overnight hours as a ridge moves through the \nGreat Lakes. A trough will sweep north of the region Sunday evening \ninto Monday that will bring a cold front Sunday evening. Ahead of \nthe front, flow will shift to be out of the southwest bringing with \nmoisture advection from the gulf. Rain showers will begin midday \nSunday ahead of the front and progress west to east with the bulk of \nthe rain out by late Sunday evening. Rainfall totals will be less \nthan from Thursday, generally around 0.25 inches or less. Behind the \ncold front with the northwesterly flow, 850mb temperatures will drop \ndown to around -2 to -6C and lake effect showers will be possible \nacross NE OH and NW PA into Monday. There will be potential for a \nwet snow fall as well Monday morning before temperatures rise, so \nnot anticipating any significant accumulation at this time. \n\nRidging and high pressure move into the region late Monday into \nTuesday and dry weather with seasonable temperatures are expected \nthrough the middle of the week.",
